{
  "rules": [
    "When the model forecasts a steady linear uptrend (e.g., +0.03-0.05 per day) while historical volatility is high, apply a mean reversion correction factor of -15% to -25% on the first 7 days of the forecast horizon.",
    "If sentiment history shows isolated spikes (0.20-0.50) followed by immediate returns to 0.00, reduce the model's projected daily gain by 40% to account for 'fake news' volatility that does not sustain.",
    "When the true outcome exhibits a sharp spike (e.g., +3.00 in one day) while the model predicts a smooth curve, apply an overshoot correction: subtract 15% from the forecasted value during the first 14 days of the horizon.",
    "In high-volatility regimes where sentiment is near zero, the model tends to overestimate trend continuity; reduce the forecasted daily increment by 60% until volatility stabilizes.",
    "If the last 5 historical prices show a downward trend despite positive sentiment, apply a negative bias correction of -2.00 EUR/day to the first 10 days of the forecast."
  ]
}
